[R0573] year=2026 | venue=2026 8th International Conference on Information Science, Electrical and Automation Engineering (ISEAE) | type=CONF
TITLE: ProtocolFuzz-Agents: Multi-Agent Semantic Fuzzing and State-Aware Exploitation Discovery for Unknown Network Protocols
KEYWORDS: Protocols; Grammar; Large language models; Fuzzing; Printing; Electromagnetic induction; Electrostatic induction; Machining; Modeling; Minimization; protocol fuzzing; multi-agent LLM; state machine inference; semantic mutation; vulnerability attribution; black-box testing; network security
ABSTRACT: Proprietary network protocols—prevalent in enterprise middleware, IIoT devices, and embedded systems—resist effective security testing because existing fuzzers either ignore message structure constraints (achieving valid-message ratios below 10%) or require protocol specifications unavailable for black-box targets. State-aware fuzzers improve coverage but require source instrumentation; LLM-augmented approaches enhance message validity in isolation but lack the session-context awareness needed to reach security-critical deep states. We present ProtocolFuzz-Agents, a four-agent cooperative framework that inductively infers message grammar and session state machines from passive traffic captures, applies constraint-preserving semantic mutations near high-value states, and produces deduplicated, interpreter-grade vulnerability reports with one-click reproducible proof-of-concept scripts. The Packet Induction Agent employs entropy-based segmentation and LLM-assisted type annotation to construct protocol grammars from raw traffic; the State Machine Agent infers session automata via DBSCAN-based response clustering and shortest-divergence exploration; the Semantic Mutation Agent generates four levels of constraint-preserving mutations targeting high-value states; and the Attribution Agent isolates causal fields through differential trace analysis and minimizes proofs-of-concept via delta debugging. The four agents operate on a shared blackboard with closed-loop feedback, enabling grammar refinement driven by mutation effectiveness and crash attribution driven by state context. On four real-world protocol stacks—including two fully proprietary binary protocols—ProtocolFuzz-Agents achieves a mean valid-message ratio of 70.2%, discovers 1.9× more unique bugs than the best prior method in 24-hour campaigns, reduces proof-of-concept minimization time by 9.1× (from 612 s to 67 s), and reproduces all three target CVEs with statistically significant improvements (Mann-Whitney U test, p < 0.01) and human-expert-rated explanation quality of 4.6/5.

